k is low, but run the full pool unit suite and test-integration.sh -rs on HEAD's tree and record the counts before the PR leaves draft.", "files": ["packages/core/src/threetears/core/shared_arrow.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A worker that dies between jobs leaves SharedArrowPool broken for good: every later run raises BrokenProcessPool", "recommendation": "instance (pre-existing, in the broken-pool path this change works on). When no job is pending, the executor's manager still hears the death and marks the executor _broken, but no job future fails, so run() never reaches its `except BrokenProcessPool` (shared_arrow.py:2580-2588) and _stale stays False. The next _submit calls pool.submit on the broken executor (shared_arrow.py:2666), which raises BrokenProcessPool from inside _submit; run() awaits it at shared_arrow.py:2574, outside the handler that sets _stale, so nothing ever replaces the pool. The same path makes a submission that races a mid-job death fail a job that never ran. The new reap never fires on that path either (no job future), so a version an idle worker mapped waits for an unrelated sweep, contrary to the new docs line. Fix: when pool.submit raises BrokenProcessPool, replace the pool under _lock and submit once more (or mark it stale and let the caller's retry find a fresh one), and sweep after the reap. Add a test that kills an idle worker (pid from jobs.process_id) and then runs a job.", "files": ["packages/core/src/threetears/core/shared_arrow.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "CHANGELOG Unreleased has no entry for the shared_arrow fix or for part 4's integration-on-the-pool changes, and the PR body says it covers each part", "recommendation": "instance: add entries. (1) Fixed in 3tears: a dead worker's hand-off, version or result left in shared memory until an unrelated sweep is now swept once the broken pool is reaped (#1140). (2) Part 4